  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nPressure rises behind this morning's cold front will continue \nbringing breezy northwest winds across much of the area, with \ngusts of 25 to 40 mph (greatest over the north and east). Weak \nconvection off of the southern Beartooths is shown by models later\nthis afternoon, the chance is low and the only concerns would be \na few lightning strikes and erratic wind gusts. A Red Flag Warning\nremains in effect for portions of southeastern MT until 9 PM this\nevening, with hot, dry, and windy conditions. Within the warning,\nthe main concerns for elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions exist along and north of Interstate 94 and US Highway \n12. Please take care not to cause a spark.\n\nRidging begins to build over the region on Tuesday. Temperatures \nwill be slightly cooler than today, though still above normal with\nhighs in the 70s to 80F. As a low continues moving east through \nthe Dakotas, lingering breezy northwest winds are forecast along \nthe Dakotas border with gusts of 15 to 30 mph. Dry conditions will\nprevail into Wednesday afternoon.\n\nThe high amplitude ridge axis moves over Montana on Wednesday, \nbringing well above normal temperatures (15 to 30F), with highs in\nthe 80s to low 90sF. Along with these temperatures, dry \nconditions will persist into the afternoon. The latest Heat Risk \nis low, however be prepared for the warmest temperatures of the \nyear and make sure to stay hydrated! Southerly flow looks to \nincrease over the for much of the day with winds gusting in the \n20s and 30s mph. The greatest gusts are forecast in the east and \nthe western mountains and valleys. Models are still working out \ndiscrepancies on the timing of an approaching trough, but the \nlatest timeframe looks to be Wednesday afternoon/evening and into \nThursday. Currently, energy on the lee side of the trough is \ndepicted moving across the west, bringing increasing southwest \nwinds with gusts into the 40s mph. Soundings show modest \ninstability of a few hundred CAPE and lots of DCAPE. With this, \nthe main concern is dry thunderstorms capable of producing strong \nand erratic wind gusts. Although many locations are still in the \ngreen-up, areas of elevated fire concerns are possible on \nWednesday with these conditions.\n\nThe latest ensembles generally show this trough becoming an upper\nlow that moves through MT into southern Canada on Thursday. The \nlatest forecast and trends have a 10-40% chance of precipitation \nin the west, with much of the forecast area missing out. To rub \nsalt into the wound, the low sliding north of the area would bring\ngusty to strong westerly winds across the area. Wind gusts of 40 \nto 55 mph are forecast, with the strongest gusts over the plains \nand foothills west of Billings. Elevated fire conditions will \nagain be possible with these strong winds, warmth, and dryness.\n\nThe general pattern looks to become unsettled Friday through the \nweekend, with a few waves bringing the chance of showers and \nthunderstorms. High temperatures are forecast to be in the 60s and\n70sF Thursday through Saturday.\n\nMatos",
